    rdfs:label "The fourth subject is control shared among keys and among people: multisignature signing and key loss, the odds of loss and theft, the bytes a script costs, and secret sharing, which the chapter does not mention (Antonopoulos ..."@en .

pg:Q8_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The fourth subject is control shared among keys and among people: multisignature signing and key loss, the odds of loss and theft, the bytes a script costs, and secret sharing, which the chapter does not mention (Antonopoulos ..."@en .

pg:Q8_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "This section is about the words, the seed and the master key that follow from them, tested against the published vectors (Palatinus et al., 2013)."@en .

pg:Q8_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Whether a scheme of several keys is safer than a single key depends on whether one fears loss or theft, and the arithmetic of the binomial distribution shows the trade (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q9_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Because a multisignature address depends on the set of keys, wallets must agree on their order, and BIP 67 solves it by sorting the public keys lexicographically by their binary form (Kerin et al., 2015)."@en .

pg:Q9_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "This section is about the words, the seed and the master key that follow from them, tested against the published vectors (Palatinus et al., 2013)."@en .

pg:Q9_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The fourth subject is control shared among keys and among people: multisignature signing and key loss, the odds of loss and theft, the bytes a script costs, and secret sharing, which the chapter does not mention (Antonopoulos ..."@en .

pg:SectionTree a ipo:TreeNavigation,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Section tree"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13/document> ;
    skos:definition "The section tree lists the sections of the chapter by their three levels, and choosing one opens that single section."@en .

pg:Sidebar a ipo:Sidebar,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Chapter explorer"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13/document> ;
    skos:definition "The explorer at the left shows the chapter's sections as a tree and keeps pace with the tabs of the main area."@en .

pg:SourceDocument a ipo:SourceDocument,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Chapter 13 Stage 3 document"@en ;
    ipo:hasLearningObjective pg:CO1,
        pg:CO2,
        pg:CO3,
        pg:CO4,
        pg:CO5,
        pg:CO6 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13/document> ;
    skos:definition "The Stage 3 document of chapter 13 is the text this page renders, section for section."@en .

pg:TopMenu a ipo:PrimaryNavigation,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Main menu"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13/document> ;
    skos:definition "The main menu is permanently visible at the top and lets the reader reach every top-level area without scrolling or opening anything first."@en .

pg:Q10_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The book says that the families of Bitcoin users are often unaware of the funds and suggests sharing access with a trusted relative or lawyer and, for a more complex scheme, multisignature access and estate planning ..."@en .

pg:Q1_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The chapter's first claim is that a Bitcoin payment can travel over an open channel because it authorizes one value to one recipient and reveals no secret, in contrast to a card number that lets anyone charge again ..."@en .

pg:Q2_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A signature is not a stamp on a blank page but a seal over the exact content, so changing the content breaks it (Lau and Wuille, 2016)."@en .

pg:Q3_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Cold storage in the book means keys generated and kept offline, and the practical question is how an online computer can then receive and prepare payments without ever holding the keys (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q4_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A partially signed bitcoin transaction is a container that travels between a machine that knows the coins and a machine that holds the keys, and BIP 174 names the roles of Creator, Updater, Signer and Finalizer (Chow, 2017)."@en .

pg:Q5_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The book warns that the bigger risk is not theft but losing access, and the word list of BIP 39 is the everyday answer, because words are easier to write down and check than a long number (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q6_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "From the words to the keys the path is a stretch and a tree: PBKDF2 turns the phrase into a seed, and BIP 32 turns the seed into a master key (Palatinus et al., 2013)."@en .

pg:Q7_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The book recommends multisignature addresses for large amounts, with keys in different places and under different people, so that no one person can compromise the funds (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q8_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Because a multisignature address depends on the set of keys, wallets must agree on their order, and BIP 67 solves it by sorting the public keys lexicographically by their binary form (Kerin et al., 2015)."@en .

pg:Q9_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The book says that many early exchanges kept all user funds in one hot wallet on one server and that many of them were hacked, and Mt."@en .

pg:S_AssumeValidShortcut a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The assumevalid shortcut"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#AssumeValidShortcut" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_AssumeValidShortcut> ;
    skos:definition "The book says that the only thing explicitly trusted is a fully validated blockchain, but Bitcoin Core 31.1 ships a second anchor: defaultAssumeValid is block 938,343 (00000000000000000000cceb...), mined on 2026-02-25 21:32:53 UTC, 33,237 blocks below the tip of 971,580 on the day of the fetch, and the node by default assumes that block and its ancestors are valid and potentially skips their script verification, unless it is started with -assumevalid=0."@en .

pg:S_BackupRangeCoverage a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "What a backup range covers"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BackupRangeCoverage" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_BackupRangeCoverage> ;
    skos:definition "A backup restores only the addresses that it already contains: with a key pool of 3 the backup covers address numbers 0 to 2, so of a coin paid to address 1 and two coins paid to address 9 only 1 of the 3 coins comes back, and a pool of 10 would have covered all 3."@en .

pg:S_Bip32MasterVector a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The BIP 32 master vector"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Bip32MasterVector" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_Bip32MasterVector> ;
    skos:definition "From the seed 000102030405060708090a0b0c0d0e0f the master key is HMAC-SHA512 keyed with the text Bitcoin seed, the left 32 bytes (starting e8f32e723decf405) are the private key and the right 32 bytes the chain code, and the Base58Check form xprv9s21ZrQH143K3QTDL4LXw2F7HEK3wJUD2nW2nRk4stbPy6cq3jPPqjiChkVvvNKmPGJxWUtg6LnF5kejMRNNU3TGtRBeJgk33yuGBxrMPHi is the one printed in BIP 32."@en .

pg:S_Bip39MnemonicChecksum a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The BIP 39 checksum"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Bip39MnemonicChecksum" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_Bip39MnemonicChecksum> ;
    skos:definition "A BIP 39 phrase of 12 words is 132 bits, 128 of entropy and 4 of checksum: the 16 zero bytes of the first test vector give abandon eleven times and about, the library reproduces that exactly, the list has 2,048 words, and replacing the last word by zoo or abandon makes the phrase invalid."@en .

pg:S_Bip39SeedFromWords a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The seed from the words"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Bip39SeedFromWords" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_Bip39SeedFromWords> ;
    skos:definition "The seed is PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA512 and 2048 rounds over the words, with the salt mnemonic plus the optional passphrase: with the passphrase TREZOR the library reproduced the 64-byte seed and the master xprv of the Trezor test vector, and without the passphrase the seed begins 5eb00bbddcf06908, a completely different wallet."@en .

pg:S_BitfinexHaircut a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The Bitfinex haircut"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BitfinexHaircut" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_BitfinexHaircut> ;
    skos:definition "After the 2016 hack Bitfinex reduced the balance of every customer by 36 percent and paid the loss back in tokens, so an account of 40 bitcoin kept 25.6 and had 14.4 turned into tokens."@en .

pg:S_BitfinexSegregatedWallets a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Bitfinex segregated wallets"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BitfinexSegregatedWallets" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_BitfinexSegregatedWallets> ;
    skos:definition "Bitfinex announced a hack in August 2016 in which 119,756 bitcoin were stolen from users' segregated wallets, after it had partnered with BitGo in 2015 for secured wallets, and the trading price fell by 20%, which shows that a custodian's wallet design can fail even when it is advertised as secured."@en .

pg:S_BookSaysNoEncryption a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The book says no encryption"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BookSaysNoEncryption" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_BookSaysNoEncryption> ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that no encryption is required for Bitcoin traffic, and its chapter 10 says that Bitcoin Core communicates entirely in the clear at the time of writing; the release notes of 26.0 added the BIP 324 v2 transport switched off, those of 27.0 say it is now enabled by default, the help text of 31.1 says -v2transport (default: 1), and the first test node listed P2P_V2 among its services."@en .

pg:S_BrainwalletCrackTime a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The brainwallet cracking time"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BrainwalletCrackTime" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_BrainwalletCrackTime> ;
    skos:definition "A secret made of four words chosen from a list of 2,048 has 2 to the power 44 possibilities, which a machine trying a trillion guesses per second (an assumed rate, not a measurement) exhausts in 17.6 seconds, while 128 bits are 2 to the power 84 times more, which is why a brainwallet from a memorized phrase is attacked with dictionaries and a seed is generated at random."@en .

pg:S_BrainwalletGuessTime a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Guessing time of a phrase"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BrainwalletGuessTime" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_BrainwalletGuessTime> ;
    skos:definition "A phrase of 4 words from a list of 2,048 has 2 to the power 44, 17,592,186,044,416, possibilities, and at an assumed rate of 10 to the power 12 guesses a second (an assumption, not a measurement) they are exhausted in 17.6 seconds, while each added word multiplies the work by 2,048."@en .

pg:S_CasasciusHologram a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The Casascius hologram"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CasasciusHologram" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_CasasciusHologram> ;
    skos:definition "Casascius coins were metal coins with a private key on paper under a tamper-evident hologram, sold until 26 November 2013 in 1, 10, 25, 100 and 1000 BTC sizes; reading the key without destroying the hologram was difficult or impossible, and FinCEN told the maker that minting physical bitcoins made him a money transmitter, which ended the sales."@en .

pg:S_Codex32AndSlip39 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Codex32 and SLIP39"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Codex32AndSlip39" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_Codex32AndSlip39> ;
    skos:definition "The chapter names multisignature but never secret sharing: BIP 93 (codex32, status Draft) splits a BIP 32 seed into up to 31 shares, the book's chapter 5 lists SLIP39 as a successor to BIP39, and the word Shamir does not occur in chapter 13."@en .

pg:S_ColdStorage a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Cold storage"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ColdStorage" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_ColdStorage> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about keeping the keys on a machine that does not go online, and the PSBT that carries a payment to it and back (Chow, 2017)."@en .

pg:S_CoreHasNoMnemonics a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Core has no mnemonics"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoreHasNoMnemonics" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_CoreHasNoMnemonics> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core does not use BIP 39 words: none of seven saved Core files (the backup, wallet and encrypt RPC sources, the managing-wallets, descriptors and PSBT documents and the command line help) mentions a mnemonic or BIP 39, while the book's chapter 5 does mention SLIP39, so recovery in Core is by backupwallet files and descriptors."@en .

pg:S_CrypterParameters a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The encryption parameters"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CrypterParameters" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_CrypterParameters> ;
    skos:definition "The master key of the wallet is encrypted with AES-256-CBC under a key of 32 bytes, with a salt of 8 bytes and an IV of 16 bytes, derived by SHA-512 rounds that default to 25000, a number the source says was just under 0.1 seconds on a 1.86 GHz Pentium M, which is 12.2 times the 2048 rounds of BIP 39."@en .

pg:S_CustodyHistory a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Custody history"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CustodyHistory" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_CustodyHistory> ;
    skos:definition "The fifth subject is the history of custody: an exchange that held a country's worth of coins, another that called its wallets secured, and a coin with a sticker (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_DescriptorIsPartOfBackup a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The descriptor is part of the backup"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DescriptorIsPartOfBackup" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_DescriptorIsPartOfBackup> ;
    skos:definition "The book's warning about backing up more than the seed is true in the node: a wallet that held one of the three keys had no idea of the multisig address (balance 0.0), a wallet given the full descriptor again found the 2.0 coins, and the same three keys in a different order under multi gave another address and balance 0.0."@en .

pg:S_DowngradeToV1 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Downgrade to v1"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DowngradeToV1" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_DowngradeToV1> ;
    skos:definition "Encryption is opportunistic: a node started with -v2transport=0 did not list P2P_V2 among its services, connected to the same listening node as a v1 peer with an empty session id, and so the listening node showed one v2 peer and one v1 peer at the same moment, the downgrade that BIP 324 says an attacker must perform to keep eavesdropping."@en .

pg:S_EncryptionMakesNewSeed a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Encryption makes a new seed"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#EncryptionMakesNewSeed" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_EncryptionMakesNewSeed> ;
    skos:definition "Encrypting a wallet makes a new HD seed and flushes the keypool: the RPC answered wallet encrypted; The keypool has been flushed and a new HD seed was generated, the 32 bytes of the old key were in the wallet file before (True) and not after (False) in this run, the file grew from 24,576 to 36,864 bytes, and the source and the managing-wallets document both say to make a new backup at once."@en .

pg:S_ExchangeFailures a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Exchange failures"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ExchangeFailures" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_ExchangeFailures> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about custodians that lost customer coins and what the book draws from them (Wikipedia contributors, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_ExternalSignerSupport a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "External signer support"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ExternalSignerSupport" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_ExternalSignerSupport> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core can be started with -signer=<cmd> so that an external tool, for example HWI, signs for a hardware device; the Bitcoin Wiki says the keys are generated by the device and never leave it, and the book says a signing device only needs to hold keys and use them to generate signatures."@en .

pg:S_GenerateblockRefusesBadSignature a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Generateblock refuses a bad signature"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#GenerateblockRefusesBadSignature" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_GenerateblockRefusesBadSignature> ;
    skos:definition "A node validates what it is asked to mine: generateblock with the address-swapped transaction answered error code -25, TestBlockValidity failed: block-script-verify-flag-failed (Script evaluated without error but finished with a false/empty top stack element), while the same call with the correct transaction returned a block hash."@en .

pg:S_GenesisHashHardCoded a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The genesis hash is hard-coded"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#GenesisHashHardCoded" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_GenesisHashHardCoded> ;
    skos:definition "The one block a node takes without proof is typed into its source: chainparams.cpp asserts that the mainnet genesis hash is 000000000019d6689c085ae165831e934ff763ae46a2a6c172b3f1b60a8ce26f, the genesis block of the saved explorer data has exactly that id at height 0, and the regtest genesis of the test node, 0f9188f1..., with no previous block, is typed into the same file, which is the book's sentence that a validated chain uses the genesis block as its root of trust."@en .

pg:S_HardwareDoesNotCheckTheAddress a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The device does not check the address"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HardwareDoesNotCheckTheAddress" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_HardwareDoesNotCheckTheAddress> ;
    skos:definition "A hardware wallet protects the key and not the intention: the Bitcoin Wiki warns that malware on a PC could monitor for high-value transactions and swap out the recipient's address, so the person must read the address on the device's own screen, and the same page lists Trezor first among the commercial hardware wallets, in chronological order."@en .

pg:S_HeirWaitInTime a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The heir's wait in time"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HeirWaitInTime" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_HeirWaitInTime> ;
    skos:definition "A relative wait of 10 counts either 10 blocks, about 100 minutes at 10 minutes a block, or 10 units of 512 seconds, 85 minutes, and the units were chosen because blocks come every 600 seconds on average."@en .

pg:S_HeirWaitsTenBlocks a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The heir waits ten blocks"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HeirWaitsTenBlocks" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_HeirWaitsTenBlocks> ;
    skos:definition "A script can give an heir a delayed path: with or(owner, and(heir, older 10)) the owner's spend was accepted at once, the heir's spend with sequence 10 was refused as non-BIP68-final at 1 and at 9 confirmations and accepted at 10, and the heir without the sequence set could not even finish signing."@en .

pg:S_Heirs a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Heirs"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Heirs" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_Heirs> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about a delayed path for an heir and the numbers that limit the delay (Friedenbach et al., 2015)."@en .

pg:S_KeepingAccess a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Keeping access"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#KeepingAccess" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_KeepingAccess> ;
    skos:definition "The third subject is the opposite risk to theft, losing access: the seed words and the arithmetic behind them, the backup file, the stale backup and the descriptor that completes it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_KeyLossTolerance a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Key loss tolerance"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#KeyLossTolerance" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_KeyLossTolerance> ;
    skos:definition "With one of three keys lost, the other two still signed the PSBT to completion; with two lost, the remaining key left one signature and no complete transaction, which is the redundancy the book mentions for a person who keeps several keys in different places."@en .

pg:S_Limits a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Limits of the experiments"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Limits" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_Limits> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about what the experiments did not do (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_LockedWalletRefuses a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A locked wallet refuses"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LockedWalletRefuses" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_LockedWalletRefuses> ;
    skos:definition "An encrypted and locked wallet still shows its balance of 1.0 coin, hands out addresses and lists public descriptors, but sending and listing private descriptors both fail with error code -13, Please enter the wallet passphrase with walletpassphrase first, and unlocked_until reads 0."@en .

pg:S_LostSevenThousand a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The lost seven thousand"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LostSevenThousand" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_LostSevenThousand> ;
    skos:definition "The book says that in July 2011 a well-known Bitcoin awareness and education project lost almost 7,000 bitcoin by losing the encryption keys of its backups; of the 77 saved sources only the book contains that sentence, so the event and the project are unconfirmed here and the lesson, not the name, is what the chapter can support."@en .

pg:S_MtGoxLoss a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The Mt. Gox loss"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MtGoxLoss" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_MtGoxLoss> ;
    skos:definition "Mt. Gox, based in Shibuya, Tokyo, handled over 70% of all bitcoin transactions by early 2014 and closed after losing 744,408 bitcoins in a theft that went undetected for years (the Bitcoin Wiki says 640 thousand were lost); its loss of 850,000 fell to 650,000 when 199,999.99 were found in an old wallet, in June 2011 fraudulent trades had pushed its price down to one cent, and the Wikipedia article names its hot wallet, which fits the book's warning about exchanges that concentrate user funds in a single hot wallet on one server."@en .

pg:S_MtGoxShare a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The Mt. Gox share of supply"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MtGoxShare" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_MtGoxShare> ;
    skos:definition "The 850,000 bitcoin that Mt. Gox reported lost, almost 750,000 of customers and about 100,000 of its own, were called around 7 percent of all bitcoins, which implies a supply of about 12.1 million coins at the time."@en .

pg:S_Multisig a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Multisignature"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Multisig" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_Multisig> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about several keys for one payment: the signing, the order of keys, the loss of keys, the odds and the cost in bytes (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_MultisigCostsBytes a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Multisig costs bytes"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MultisigCostsBytes" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_MultisigCostsBytes> ;
    skos:definition "Safety has a price in bytes: the same one-input one-output payment took 110 virtual bytes from a single key and 146 from the 2-of-3 script, 36 more, a ratio of 1.33, which at 10 satoshis per virtual byte is 360 satoshis more."@en .

pg:S_MultisigVsizeFormula a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The multisig size by hand"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MultisigVsizeFormula" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_MultisigVsizeFormula> ;
    skos:definition "The virtual size of a one-input one-output spend of a 2-of-3 script to a native segwit address can be counted by hand: 82 bytes without witness weigh 328 units, the witness holds the 2 marker bytes, the item count, an empty item, 2 signatures of 73 bytes, the script length and the 105-byte script, 584 units in all, which is 146 virtual bytes and, at 10 satoshis per virtual byte, 1,460 satoshis, the number that the regtest transaction measured."@en .

pg:S_OddsOfLossAndTheft a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The odds of loss and theft"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#OddsOfLossAndTheft" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_OddsOfLossAndTheft> ;
    skos:definition "If each key is lost with probability 0.1 and stolen with 0.05, independently (an assumption), one key alone is lost with 0.1 and stolen with 0.05; 2-of-3 is stuck with 0.028 and robbed with 0.00725; 1-of-3 is stuck with 0.001 but robbed with 0.14263; and at a loss chance of 0.5 the 2-of-3 stands exactly where the single key does, 0.5."@en .

pg:S_OlderSixtyFiveThousand a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The limit of older"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#OlderSixtyFiveThousand" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_OlderSixtyFiveThousand> ;
    skos:definition "BIP 68 reads only the low 16 bits of the sequence, so a wait is at most 65,535 blocks (about 455.1 days) or 65,535 units of 512 seconds (33,553,920 seconds, 388.4 days), and the descriptor parser of Bitcoin Core 31.1 accepted both older(65535) and older(65536), but the heir whose key was behind older(65536) could spend with 1 confirmation, because 65536 has no low 16 bits."@en .

pg:S_OneShareFitsEverySecret a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "One share fits every secret"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#OneShareFitsEverySecret" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_OneShareFitsEverySecret> ;
    skos:definition "Over the prime 11, a single share at x = 1 with the value 5 fits 11 pairs of secret and slope, one for each of the 11 possible secrets, so the share alone excludes nothing."@en .

pg:S_OneShareRevealsNothing a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "One share reveals nothing"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#OneShareRevealsNothing" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_OneShareRevealsNothing> ;
    skos:definition "A single share leaves the secret completely undetermined: over the small prime 257 the share (1, 100) fits 257 pairs of secret and slope, one for each of the 257 possible secrets and 257 different slopes, which is Shamir's condition that any k minus 1 pieces leave the data completely undetermined."@en .

pg:S_PaperAndMemory a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Paper and memory"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PaperAndMemory" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_PaperAndMemory> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about two old ways of keeping a key, on paper and in the head, and why both have lost favour (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_PaperWalletsAreObsolete a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Paper wallets are obsolete"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PaperWalletsAreObsolete" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_PaperWalletsAreObsolete> ;
    skos:definition "The book recommends a seed printed on paper as a backup, but the Bitcoin Wiki now calls the paper wallet an obsolete and unsafe method that was popular between 2011 and 2016, because printers often have a hard drive that keeps the file; the distinction is a single private key on paper against a seed on paper."@en .

pg:S_PhysicalCoins a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Physical coins"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PhysicalCoins" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_PhysicalCoins> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about bitcoin made physical, with a key under a hologram (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_PinWaitByAttempts a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "PIN waits by attempts"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PinWaitByAttempts" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_PinWaitByAttempts> ;
    skos:definition "The waits of a PIN that doubles after each failed try, 1, 2, 4 seconds and so on, add up to 32,767 seconds or 9.1 hours before the sixteenth attempt, and the sum is 2 to the power of the number of waits minus 1, so every extra try before the wipe doubles the thief's total waiting time."@en .

pg:S_ProtectingKeys a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Protecting keys"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ProtectingKeys" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_ProtectingKeys> ;
    skos:definition "The second subject is how a key is protected where it lives: offline, behind a passphrase, inside a device, on paper or in a memory (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_PsbtThroughColdNode a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A PSBT through a cold node"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PsbtThroughColdNode" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_PsbtThroughColdNode> ;
    skos:definition "A cold wallet can sign without a blockchain: the cold node had 0 blocks and 0 connections, took the unsigned PSBT of 528 characters, returned a signed PSBT of 596 characters with the final witness, and the hot node finalized and broadcast it, giving 1 confirmation and 1.0 coin received, the Creator, Updater, Signer, Finalizer flow of BIP 174; a second computer is not an air gap, and Wikipedia notes that viruses such as Stuxnet were designed to infect air-gapped systems through removable media."@en .

pg:S_ReplayPaysNothing a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A replay pays nothing"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ReplayPaysNothing" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_ReplayPaysNothing> ;
    skos:definition "Sending an old payment again moves no more money: after the transaction was in a block, sendrawtransaction answered error code -27, Transaction outputs already in utxo set, testmempoolaccept said txn-already-known, the receiving wallet still held 1.5 coins before and after another block, and the same answer came after the next block, unlike a card number that can be charged again and again."@en .

pg:S_RestoreGivesSameAddresses a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Restore gives the same addresses"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RestoreGivesSameAddresses" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_RestoreGivesSameAddresses> ;
    skos:definition "A file made by backupwallet and restored by restorewallet gave the same wallet: the source wallet and the restored one both held 3.0 coins, the next new address of the restored wallet was the address the source handed out next, and the address far along the chain belonged to it."@en .

pg:S_RobbedChance a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The chance of being robbed"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RobbedChance" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_RobbedChance> ;
    skos:definition "If each of 3 keys is stolen with a chance of 5 percent, independently (an assumption), a scheme that needs 2 of them is robbed whenever 2 or more keys are stolen, with a chance of 0.00725, against 0.05 for a single key."@en .

pg:S_RootOfTrust a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The root of trust"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RootOfTrust" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_RootOfTrust> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the single block that a node trusts, the check that it applies to its own blocks and the second anchor of the software (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_SecurityModel a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The security model"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SecurityModel" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_SecurityModel> ;
    skos:definition "The first subject is the security model of the chapter: why a payment can be public, why the network may be open, which single block a node takes on trust, and how a user checks the software before running it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_SeedWords a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Seed words"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SeedWords" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_SeedWords> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the words, the seed and the master key that follow from them, tested against the published vectors (Palatinus et al., 2013)."@en .

pg:S_SequenceMaskArithmetic a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The sequence mask arithmetic"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SequenceMaskArithmetic" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_SequenceMaskArithmetic> ;
    skos:definition "BIP 68 keeps 16 bits of the sequence number, so the largest wait is 65,535 blocks, about 455.1 days, and the value 65,536 has no bits in the mask and means a wait of 0."@en .

pg:S_ShamirTwoOfThree a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Shamir two-of-three"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ShamirTwoOfThree" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_ShamirTwoOfThree> ;
    skos:definition "Adi Shamir's scheme hides a secret as the constant term of a random polynomial: with a degree-1 polynomial over the prime 2 to the 127 minus 1, the secret 123456789 and the slope 987654321 gave the shares 1111111110, 2098765431 and 3086419752, any two of them recovered the secret, and one share did not."@en .

pg:S_ShareGroups a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Groups that open a secret"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ShareGroups" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_ShareGroups> ;
    skos:definition "In a scheme that needs 3 of 5 shares, the groups that can open the secret are all 16 groups of 3, 4 or 5 holders, while the 10 groups of 2 holders learn nothing."@en .

pg:S_SharingASecret a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Sharing a secret"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SharingASecret" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_SharingASecret> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about splitting a secret into shares, from Shamir's paper of 1979 to the formats of today (Shamir, 1979)."@en .

pg:S_SharingControl a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Sharing control"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SharingControl" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_SharingControl> ;
    skos:definition "The fourth subject is control shared among keys and among people: multisignature signing and key loss, the odds of loss and theft, the bytes a script costs, and secret sharing, which the chapter does not mention (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_SighashAllIsTheDefault a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "SIGHASH_ALL is the default"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SighashAllIsTheDefault" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_SighashAllIsTheDefault> ;
    skos:definition "The promise that a signed payment cannot be changed holds for the default signature type: the wallet's signature ended with the byte 01, SIGHASH_ALL, which commits to every output, while the interpreter of Bitcoin Core 31.1 also knows NONE, SINGLE and ANYONECANPAY, and BIP 143 says that hashOutputs commits to all outputs only when the type is neither SINGLE nor NONE."@en .

pg:S_SignatureNeedsKeys a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A signature needs keys"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SignatureNeedsKeys" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_SignatureNeedsKeys> ;
    skos:definition "A matching hash proves nothing if the list itself came from the attacker: SHA256SUMS.asc holds 11 detached signatures of the builders, gpg found all 11 of them and could call none of them good with an empty keyring, exit code 2 and 11 times No public key, and the Guix instructions of Bitcoin Core say the aim is to reproduce the toolchain instead of blindly trusting binary downloads."@en .

pg:S_SignedTransactionHidesKey a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A signed transaction hides the key"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SignedTransactionHidesKey" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_SignedTransactionHidesKey> ;
    skos:definition "A signed transaction contains the signature and the public key and never the private key: on the test chain the spending input had two witness items of 71 and 33 bytes, the 33 bytes were the public key, the 32-byte private key and its WIF text were not in the 222 bytes of the transaction, and the book's sentence is that a transaction authorizes only a specific value to a specific recipient and cannot be forged."@en .

pg:S_SigningDevices a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Signing devices"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SigningDevices" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_SigningDevices> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about devices that hold the keys: the signer interface of the node, the limit of what a device can protect and the PIN wait (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_SmallShamirByHand a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Shamir by hand"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SmallShamirByHand" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_SmallShamirByHand> ;
    skos:definition "With the prime 47, the secret 12 and the slope 30, the shares at x = 1, 2 and 3 are 42, 25 and 8, and the first two give the secret back as 2 times 42 minus 25, which is 12 modulo 47."@en .

pg:S_SortedmultiOrder a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The order in sortedmulti"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SortedmultiOrder" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_SortedmultiOrder> ;
    skos:definition "The order of the keys in sortedmulti does not matter: the three public keys in two orders gave the same address, while multi with the same keys in two orders gave different addresses, the wallet's first address was the derived address 0, and BIP 67 says to sort the keys lexicographically by their binary form."@en .

pg:S_StaleBackupMissesFunds a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A stale backup misses funds"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#StaleBackupMissesFunds" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_StaleBackupMissesFunds> ;
    skos:definition "A backup does not know what happens after it: on a node with -keypool=3 the stale copy restored 1.0 coin of the 3.0, because the later 2.0 coins went to the tenth address, outside the stale range 0 to 2, while the help text of the option warns that smaller sizes may increase the risk of losing funds when restoring from an old backup; the default size is 1000."@en .

pg:S_StretchingBuysDays a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Stretching buys days"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#StretchingBuysDays" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_StretchingBuysDays> ;
    skos:definition "At the rate that the source gives for the default stretch of 25,000 rounds, just under 0.1 seconds on a 1.86 GHz Pentium M, a guesser who has to try all 2 to the power 30 passphrases of 30 bits needs about 1,242.8 days on one such machine, and every added bit of passphrase doubles that time, which is the only protection a short passphrase gets."@en .

pg:S_StuckChance a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The chance of being stuck"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#StuckChance" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_StuckChance> ;
    skos:definition "If each of 3 keys is lost with a chance of 10 percent, independently (an assumption), a scheme that needs 2 of them is stuck whenever fewer than 2 keys survive, with a chance of 0.028, against 0.1 for a single key."@en .

pg:S_Survivability a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Survivability"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Survivability" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_Survivability> ;
    skos:definition "The sixth subject is availability after the key holder is gone: an heir path behind a timelock, the number that silently breaks it, and what this chapter did not run (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_TamperBreaksSignature a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Tampering breaks the signature"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TamperBreaksSignature" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_TamperBreaksSignature> ;
    skos:definition "A signature covers the outputs, so a thief who changes them invalidates it: the valid transaction was accepted by testmempoolaccept, the same transaction with the recipient's script replaced by an attacker's script, and the same transaction with its first output lowered from 1.5 to 1 coin, were each refused with mempool-script-verify-flag-failed, the script failure that follows from the hashOutputs commitment of BIP 143."@en .

pg:S_TarballHashMatches a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The tarball hash matches"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TarballHashMatches" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_TarballHashMatches> ;
    skos:definition "The binary used for every experiment of this chapter was checked against the published list: the SHA-256 of the 90,293,352-byte tarball bitcoin-31.1-x86_64-linux-gnu.tar.gz, starting b80d9c3e04da78fb, equals its line among the 28 lines of SHA256SUMS and that line is in the saved copy of the list."@en .

pg:S_TransactionsPublishable a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Transactions that can be published"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TransactionsPublishable" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_TransactionsPublishable> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about what a signed payment reveals and what happens when it is changed or sent again (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_TransportEncryption a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Transport encryption"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TransportEncryption" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_TransportEncryption> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the encrypted transport between nodes, how it differs from the book's statement and how it can be bypassed (Mehta et al., 2019)."@en .

pg:S_TrezorPinWait a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The Trezor PIN wait"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TrezorPinWait" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_TrezorPinWait> ;
    skos:definition "The PIN of a Trezor is protected by doubling waits, 1, 2, 4 seconds and so on, and a wipe after 16 failed attempts, so the waits before the sixteenth attempt add up to 32,767 seconds, 9.1 hours, a rule that makes guessing slow and finite."@en .

pg:S_TwoOfThreeSigning a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Two-of-three signing"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TwoOfThreeSigning" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_TwoOfThreeSigning> ;
    skos:definition "A 2-of-3 wallet needs two signers: the first key alone left the PSBT incomplete with 1 signature, the first and third together made it complete, it was finalized, accepted by the mempool, and the input had four witness items (an empty dummy, two signatures and the script) in 146 virtual bytes."@en .

pg:S_TypoCaught a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The chance a typo is caught"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TypoCaught" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_TypoCaught> ;
    skos:definition "A checksum of 4 bits, as in a phrase of 12 words, lets a random wrong phrase pass with a chance of 1 in 16, 0.0625, and one of 8 bits, as in 24 words, with a chance of 1 in 256, which is the modest protection that BIP 39 itself calls short."@en .

pg:S_UnlockTimeout a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The unlock timeout"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#UnlockTimeout" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_UnlockTimeout> ;
    skos:definition "walletpassphrase keeps the key in memory only for the timeout: unlocked_until was exactly 3 seconds ahead of now, a send in that window worked, after 5 seconds unlocked_until was 0 and the send failed with -13 again, walletlock gave 0 at once, and the timeout is capped at 100000000 seconds, about 3 years, in the source."@en .

pg:S_V2SessionOnBothSides a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The v2 session on both sides"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#V2SessionOnBothSides" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_V2SessionOnBothSides> ;
    skos:definition "When two Bitcoin Core 31.1 nodes connected on the loopback address, both reported the transport v2 and the same 64-hex-digit session id, the value that BIP 324 exports so that the operators of two nodes can compare it and detect a man in the middle."@en .

pg:S_VerifyingTheSoftware a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Verifying the software"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#VerifyingTheSoftware" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_VerifyingTheSoftware> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the check of the program before it is run: the hash, the signatures and the reproducible build (Bitcoin Core release signers, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_WalletBackups a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Wallet backups"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#WalletBackups" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_WalletBackups> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the backup file, its age, its completeness and the book's story of a backup that could not be opened (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_WalletEncryption a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Wallet encryption"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#WalletEncryption" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_WalletEncryption> ;
    skos:definition "This section is about the passphrase of the wallet: its errors, its timeout, its parameters and its effect on the seed (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_WatchOnlyCannotSign a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A watch-only wallet cannot sign"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#WatchOnlyCannotSign" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_WatchOnlyCannotSign> ;
    skos:definition "A watch-only wallet holds public descriptors, so it can receive and build a payment but not sign: the hot node's wallet had private keys disabled, walletprocesspsbt returned complete false with no signature added, listdescriptors with private keys failed with -4 Can't get private descriptor string for watch-only wallets, and the PSBT carried the key origin data (bip32_derivs) that the signer needs."@en .

pg:S_WhatWasNotExecuted a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "What was not executed"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#WhatWasNotExecuted" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_WhatWasNotExecuted> ;
    skos:definition "The record lists ten things that were not run in this chapter: the hardware device, a true air gap, paper and metal backups, an exchange breach, estate planning with a lawyer, mainnet funds, Shamir in a wallet, BIP 39 recovery in Core, a good gpg verification and BIP 324 on the wire, each with its reason."@en .

pg:S_WordsFromEntropy a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Words from entropy"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#WordsFromEntropy" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_WordsFromEntropy> ;
    skos:definition "BIP 39 appends the first entropy-length-divided-by-32 bits of the SHA-256 hash as checksum and cuts the sum into groups of 11 bits, so 16 bytes, 128 bits, get a 4-bit checksum and become 12 words, and 32 bytes, 256 bits, become 24."@en .

pg:S_WrongPassphraseError a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The wrong passphrase error"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#WrongPassphraseError" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13#S_WrongPassphraseError> ;
    skos:definition "A wrong passphrase gives error code -14, The wallet passphrase entered was incorrect; after walletpassphrasechange the old passphrase gave -14 as well and the new one worked, and the managing-wallets document of Bitcoin Core states that there is no way to recover a passphrase."@en .

pg:CO5 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Learn from the custody failures of Mt. Gox and Bitfinex and from physical coins what a custodian can and cannot be trusted with"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Evaluate ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can learn from the custody failures of Mt. Gox and Bitfinex and from physical coins what a custodian can and cannot be trusted with."@en .

pg:CO6 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Design a delayed path for an heir with a timelock and state the limits of the chapter's experiments, what was not executed"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can design a delayed path for an heir with a timelock and state the limits of the chapter's experiments, what was not executed."@en .

pg:CO1 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Explain what the security model of Bitcoin protects (a signed transaction can be published, the transport can be encrypted) and how a user checks the software and the root of trust"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Understand ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can explain what the security model of Bitcoin protects (a signed transaction can be published, the transport can be encrypted) and how a user checks the software and the root of trust."@en .

pg:CO2 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Compare cold storage, wallet encryption, signing devices and paper or memory as ways to protect keys, and judge what each one does and does not defend against"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can compare cold storage, wallet encryption, signing devices and paper or memory as ways to protect keys, and judge what each one does and does not defend against."@en .

pg:CO3 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Explain how seed words and wallet backups keep access to coins, including the BIP 39 checksum, the descriptor in a backup and the risk of a stale backup"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can explain how seed words and wallet backups keep access to coins, including the BIP 39 checksum, the descriptor in a backup and the risk of a stale backup."@en .

pg:CO4 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Compute the odds of loss and theft of a single key against a 2-of-3 multisignature wallet, and explain how Shamir's scheme shares a secret so that one piece reveals nothing"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch13/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can compute the odds of loss and theft of a single key against a 2-of-3 multisignature wallet, and explain how Shamir's scheme shares a secret so that one piece reveals nothing."@en .

